steps.body:
ASCII has no `é`, `€`, or `日`. **Unicode** gives every character of every script a number, its **code point**, written `U+` and at least four hex digits: `U+0041` is `A`, `U+00E9` is `é`, `U+20AC` is `€`, and `U+1F680` is 🚀. There's room for 1,114,112 code points, U+0000 to U+10FFFF, and about 150,000 are assigned.

A code point is a number, and an encoding decides its bytes. **UTF-8**, the encoding of the web, JSON, Linux, macOS, and nearly every feed, uses 1 to 4 bytes:

| Code points | Bytes | First byte | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| U+0000 to U+007F | 1 | `00` to `7F` | `A` is `41` |
| U+0080 to U+07FF | 2 | `C2` to `DF` | `é` is `C3 A9` |
| U+0800 to U+FFFF | 3 | `E0` to `EF` | `€` is `E2 82 AC` |
| U+10000 to U+10FFFF | 4 | `F0` to `F4` | 🚀 is `F0 9F 9A 80` |

Two properties make it work. ASCII text is already UTF-8, byte for byte. And every byte after the first is a **continuation byte**, `80` to `BF`, so no piece of a longer character ever looks like ASCII. Splitting UTF-8 on `,` or `|` is always safe.

g++ and clang read source files as UTF-8, so `"€"` in your code is those three bytes. `std::string` stores bytes and knows nothing about encodings: `size()` counts bytes, `s[i]` is one byte, and `std::string("Zürich").size()` is 7. To count code points, count the bytes that aren't continuation bytes:

```cpp
const std::string s = "Zürich";
int points = 0;
for (char c : s) {
    const int code = static_cast<unsigned char>(c);
    if (code < 0x80 || code >= 0xC0) {
        ++points;
    }
}
std::cout << s.size() << ' ' << points << '\n';  // 7 6
```

Bytes matter for buffers and wire formats, and code points for limits like "at most 12 characters". What a screen shows is a third count: `日` and 🚀 take two columns in a terminal. `std::setw` pads by bytes, so `std::setw(8)` gives `Zürich` one space where it needs two, and the column drifts.

